Tables from ChatGPT, Claude or Gemini are written in Markdown. Paste one into Excel and every row ends up in column A, pipes and all. So I built a free online tool that converts Markdown tables straight into Excel:
https://md-to.com/markdown-to-excel/

Paste a whole AI answer or upload a .md file, and every table is extracted automatically
Several tables become several sheets, named after the heading above each table
1,234.50 and 12.5% are saved as real numbers; IDs longer than 15 digits stay as text so Excel can't round them
Bold, frozen header row and auto-sized columns
Download an .xlsx, or copy with one click and paste into Excel
Runs entirely in the browser: no uploads, free, no signup
An .xlsx file is a zip archive with a few XML parts. Instead of SheetJS, the tool uses a small hand-written XLSX writer and zips the parts with fflate, with no new dependencies:
import { strToU8, zipSync } from 'fflate';

files['xl/styles.xml'] = strToU8(styles.toXml());
files['xl/sharedStrings.xml'] = strToU8(strings.toXml());
return zipSync(files, { level: 6 });

When building a RESTful API, securing your endpoints is just as important as the business logic itself. While implementing JWT (JSON Web Tokens) in a standard .NET application is common, doing it correctly within a Clean Architecture structure requires careful separation of concerns. You don't want your Core Domain knowing about HTTP requests, libraries, or token generation algorithms.
In this article, I'll show you how I implemented a decoupled, secure JWT authentication system in my latest .NET API, ensuring that the infrastructure logic remains completely isolated from the business rules.
The Contract (Interface)
To keep my API Controllers clean and easily testable, I rely on the Service Pattern. The controller shouldn't care about the specific packages or algorithms used to generate a token.
To achieve this, I defined a simple interface inside my Services folder.
using SarahaAPI.Models;

namespace SarahaAPI.Services
{
    public interface IJWTService
    {
        public string GenerateToken(User user);
    }
}

The Implementation (Service Layer)
Next, I implement this interface in the JWTService class. This class handles the actual token generation using the System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt library. By isolating this logic in the Service layer, the API controller only interacts with the interface, keeping it decoupled from the token generation process.




